class TurnWorld(BaseModel):
    """
    The world as the client read it at the END of one agent turn, for the simulated user's view.

    ``status`` is ``observed`` or ``unobservable``; a client that predates the field sends no
    ``turn_world`` at all, which is the third state. ``unobservable`` always names its ``reason``:
    ``not_declared`` (the config declares no ``world_state.user_surfaces``), ``no_world_route``
    (there is no world this client can read), ``read_failed`` (the read raised) or ``overflow``
    (the delta exceeded the client's size cap — it is never truncated). ``reason`` is ``null``
    iff ``status`` is ``observed``, and it is never an empty object standing for "unobservable".

    ``delta`` is set iff ``observed``: the world at the end of this agent turn minus the previous
    capture. ``seed_delta`` rides ONLY the first turn a client reports: the world after the client
    seeded it minus the ``initial_world_state`` ``/threads`` returned, so the server's fold starts
    from the world the agent actually met. ``version`` is ``1``.

    ``types`` names the entity types this capture COVERS: the row types of the surfaces the
    client's own declaration lists, and only those — a client never ships the rest of the world.
    A declared surface whose row type is not in ``types`` was not captured, and the server shows
    it as unobservable rather than as an empty screen.
    """

    model_config = ConfigDict(
        extra='ignore',
    )
    delta: WorldDelta | None = None
    reason: Annotated[
        Literal['not_declared', 'no_world_route', 'read_failed', 'overflow'] | None,
        Field(title='Reason'),
    ] = None
    seed_delta: WorldDelta | None = None
    status: Annotated[Literal['observed', 'unobservable'], Field(title='Status')]
    types: Annotated[list[str] | None, Field(title='Types')] = []
    version: Annotated[Literal[1], Field(title='Version')]


class UserTurnRequest(BaseModel):
    """
    The chatbot just spoke; ask the user agent for the next user message.

    ``agent_state`` is the agent's own STRUCTURED output for this turn, beside the reply
    text — whatever the customer's agent returns alongside its message (suggestion chips,
    a selected entity, a form state, a routing decision). Optional and additive in BOTH
    directions: an SDK that never sends it leaves judges exactly as they were, and sending
    it to a server that predates the field is ignored (``extra='ignore'``). Without this
    channel, structure that never appears in the reply text is UNGRADEABLE — no wording of
    a judge prompt can conjure it. For example, a judge that reads follow-up
    chips from ``agent_state.last_suggestions`` can grade them only when the runtime
    delivers that field.

    ``turn_messages`` is THIS turn's full agent-side chain (carrier narration, tool
    calls, tool results, final reply) — the narration channel. ``agent_response`` alone
    carries only the final reply, so without this channel per-tool-call narration is
    invisible to every judge. Same compat posture as ``agent_state``:
    optional and additive in both directions.

    SO THE AGENT SIDE NEEDS NO NEW CARRIER, AND DOES NOT GET ONE. ``turn_messages`` IS
    the list of messages an agent turn is, content parts included (``SerializedMessage.content``
    already takes a part list), and ``agent_response`` is its TEXT PROJECTION — the same
    string-is-the-projection / list-is-the-record split ``UserTurnResponse.user_message`` /
    ``user_messages`` states for the user side. A structured-only turn is therefore a turn
    whose ``agent_response`` is ``''`` and whose ``turn_messages`` carries the ``json`` part:
    LEGITIMATE, and covered by the empty-reply rule above, not a silent reply. The projection
    of a part list into judge/simulator text is the SERVER's — a client must not flatten parts into
    ``agent_response`` itself, or the record and the projection would disagree about the same
    turn in the two places that read them.

    ``agent_actions`` is what the agent DID this turn beside what it said (see
    ``AgentAction``). ``agent_response`` may legitimately be empty when this is not:
    a turn counts as delivered when EITHER is non-empty, which is the test the user
    simulator, the ``agent_reply`` sandbox accessor and the ``reply_not_visible``
    corroboration all apply. ``None`` means the client cannot observe its agent's
    actions at all (an SDK that predates the field) and an empty list means it can
    and there were none — the same tri-state posture as ``turn_tool_events``, and
    the reason this is not defaulted to ``[]``.

    ``reply_source`` says WHERE ``agent_response`` came from, as the client's
    projection labelled it: ``assistant`` (the agent's own words), ``tool_result``
    (the turn had no agent words and ended on a tool result; ``agent_response`` is
    the text of that trailing tool result, relayed because it is what the graph
    returned to its caller — or ``''`` on an older SDK), ``interrupt``, ``empty``
    (the RUNNER emptied this turn: its adapter produced nothing readable) or
    ``unknown``. The label rides the wire so that a manufactured empty turn and a
    genuinely silent agent are told apart; without it they are the same bytes here
    and the judges grade both as the agent's silence. A turn labelled ``empty`` /
    ``unknown`` / ``tool_result`` carries no agent speech; when it also carries no
    ``agent_actions``, its turn-scoped cells are recorded ``not_measured`` rather
    than red.
    Deliberately a plain ``str`` for the same additive reason ``ended_early`` is:
    the vocabulary is shared with the SDK and will grow.

    ``turn_idx`` is the client's 0-based loop index for this turn — the SDK's
    ``range(max_turns)`` counter — which is contractually equal to the server's
    per-turn key, the PRE-increment ``thread.turn_count`` this turn is filed under.
    It makes ``/user-turn`` an idempotency-keyed POST so the SDK can retry a
    read-timeout / 502 / 503 / 504 without double-driving the dialog (a single slow
    turn does not kill the whole scenario): ``turn_idx == turn_count`` drives the
    turn as usual; ``turn_idx == turn_count - 1`` means the turn was already driven
    but its response was lost in flight, so the server REPLAYS that turn's stored
    reply verbatim — it does NOT re-drive the user-sim or re-run the per-turn judges;
    any other value is a real client/server turn-cursor divergence and is answered
    with 409. Absent (an SDK that predates the field) is exactly the pre-field
    behavior — every request drives a turn, and the transport layer retries none of
    them. Same additive-in-both-directions posture as the other optional fields.

    ``turn_world`` is the world at the END of this agent turn, as a delta (``TurnWorld``), from
    which the server folds and projects what the simulated user can SEE: the rows of the
    ``world_state.user_surfaces`` the config declares. Tri-state: absent (a client that
    predates the field — the user's view is exactly what it was before), ``unobservable`` with a
    typed reason, or ``observed``. ``user_action_result`` is how the client resolved the
    ``user_action`` the PREVIOUS response asked for; absent when there was none.
    """

    model_config = ConfigDict(
        extra='ignore',
    )
    agent_actions: Annotated[list[AgentAction] | None, Field(title='Agent Actions')] = None
    agent_response: Annotated[str, Field(title='Agent Response')]
    agent_state: Annotated[dict[str, Any] | None, Field(title='Agent State')] = None
    reply_source: Annotated[str | None, Field(title='Reply Source')] = None
    turn_idx: Annotated[int | None, Field(title='Turn Idx')] = None
    turn_messages: Annotated[list[SerializedMessage] | None, Field(title='Turn Messages')] = None
    turn_world: TurnWorld | None = None
    user_action_result: UserActionResult | None = None
